Sure! Let's break down your requirements into two parts:

### Part 1: `FavoritesManager` Class

This class will handle adding and removing names from the favorites list. It will also save these entries either to the database (for logged-in users) or to the session (for guest users).

Here is how you can implement the `FavoritesManager` class:

```php
<?php

namespace App\Favorites;

use App\Models\User;
use App\Models\Favorite;
use Illuminate\Http\Request;

class FavoritesManager
{
    public function addToFavorites(Request $request)
    {
        // Check if the request has a 'name' parameter
        if ($request->has('name')) {
            $name = $request->input('name');

            // Determine whether the user is logged in or not
            if (Auth::check()) {
                // If the user is logged in, save to the database
                $user = User::find(Auth::id());
                $favorite = new Favorite();
                $favorite->name = $name;
                $favorite->user_id = $user->id;
                $favorite->save();
            } else {
                // If the user is a guest, save to the session
                session()->put('favorites', session()->get('favorites', []) ?? []);
                session()->push('favorites', $name);
            }
        }

        return response()->json(['message' => 'Name added to favorites']);
    }

    public function removeFromFavorites(Request $request)
    {
        // Check if the request has a 'name' parameter
        if ($request->has('name')) {
            $name = $request->input('name');

            // Determine whether the user is logged in or not
            if (Auth::check()) {
                // If the user is logged in, delete from the database
                $user = User::find(Auth::id());
                $favorite = Favorite::where('name', $name)->where('user_id', $user->id)->first();
                if ($favorite) {
                    $favorite->delete();
                }
            } else {
                // If the user is a guest, remove from the session
                session()->forget('favorites');
            }
        }

        return response()->json(['message' => 'Name removed from favorites']);
    }
}
```

### Part 2: `FavoritesController` Class

This class will act as an API controller to connect the Vue frontend to the `FavoritesManager` class.

Here is how you can implement the `FavoritesController` class:

```php
<?php

namespace App\Http\Controllers;

use App\Favorites\FavoritesManager;
use Illuminate\Http\Request;

class FavoritesController extends Controller
{
    protected $favoritesManager;

    public function __construct(FavoritesManager $favoritesManager)
    {
        $this->favoritesManager = $favoritesManager;
    }

    public function addToFavorites(Request $request)
    {
        return $this->favoritesManager->addToFavorites($request);
    }

    public function removeFromFavorites(Request $request)
    {
        return $this->favoritesManager->removeFromFavorites($request);
    }
}
```

### Summary

- **`FavoritesManager`**: Handles adding and removing names from the favorites list. Saves to the database for logged-in users and to the session for guest users.
- **`FavoritesController`**: Acts as an API controller to connect the Vue frontend to the `FavoritesManager` class.

These classes should provide the functionality you need for managing favorites in your Laravel application.

