Certainly! Let's create a simple example of a banking application using the Strategy Pattern in JavaScript. The strategy pattern allows us to define a family of algorithms, encapsulate each one, and make them interchangeable.

Here’s an example where we have different types of transactions (e.g., deposit, withdrawal, transfer) and each transaction type has its own logic for processing the transaction.

```javascript
// Define the base class for all strategies
class TransactionStrategy {
    executeTransaction(amount) {
        throw new Error("This method should be overridden by subclasses.");
    }
}

// Concrete strategy for depositing money
class DepositStrategy extends TransactionStrategy {
    executeTransaction(amount) {
        console.log(`Depositing ${amount} into your account.`);
    }
}

// Concrete strategy for withdrawing money
class WithdrawalStrategy extends TransactionStrategy {
    executeTransaction(amount) {
        if (amount > this.account.balance) {
            console.log("Insufficient funds.");
            return;
        }
        this.account.balance -= amount;
        console.log(`Withdrawing ${amount} from your account.`);
    }
}

// Concrete strategy for transferring money
class TransferStrategy extends TransactionStrategy {
    executeTransaction(amount, recipientAccount) {
        if (this.account.balance < amount) {
            console.log("Insufficient funds.");
            return;
        }
        this.account.balance -= amount;
        recipientAccount.balance += amount;
        console.log(`Transferring ${amount} to ${recipientAccount.name}.`);
    }
}

// Account class with a balance property
class Account {
    constructor(name, balance) {
        this.name = name;
        this.balance = balance;
    }
}

// Context class that uses the strategy pattern
class BankingApp {
    constructor(account) {
        this.account = account;
    }

    setTransactionStrategy(strategy) {
        this.transactionStrategy = strategy;
    }

    performTransaction(amount) {
        this.transactionStrategy.executeTransaction(amount);
    }
}

// Create some accounts
const account1 = new Account("John Doe", 1000);
const account2 = new Account("Jane Smith", 500);

// Set up the context with different strategies
const bankingApp1 = new BankingApp(account1);
bankingApp1.setTransactionStrategy(new DepositStrategy());
bankingApp1.performTransaction(500); // Depositing 500 into John Doe's account.

const bankingApp2 = new BankingApp(account2);
bankingApp2.setTransactionStrategy(new WithdrawalStrategy());
bankingApp2.performTransaction(300); // Withdrawing 300 from Jane Smith's account.

const bankingApp3 = new BankingApp(account1);
bankingApp3.setTransactionStrategy(new TransferStrategy());
bankingApp3.performTransaction(200, account2); // Transferring 200 from John Doe's account to Jane Smith's account.
```

In this example:
- We have three concrete strategies: `DepositStrategy`, `WithdrawalStrategy`, and `TransferStrategy`.
- Each strategy has its own implementation of the `executeTransaction` method.
- The `BankingApp` class uses these strategies through the `setTransactionStrategy` method to perform transactions on the account.

This approach makes it easy to add or modify transaction behaviors without changing the core logic of the `BankingApp` class.

