To get the bounding box of a deeply nested SVG object in JavaScript, you can use a recursive function that traverses the SVG tree and collects the coordinates of each element's bounding box. Here's an example:

```javascript
function getBoundingBox(svg) {
    const boundingBox = { x: Infinity, y: Infinity, width: 0, height: 0 };

    function traverse(element) {
        if (element.type === 'svg') {
            for (let i = 0; i < element.children.length; i++) {
                let child = element.children[i];
                if (child.type === 'g') {
                    traverse(child);
                } else {
                    let bbox = child.getBBox();
                    if (bbox.x < boundingBox.x) boundingBox.x = bbox.x;
                    if (bbox.y < boundingBox.y) boundingBox.y = bbox.y;
                    if (bbox.width > boundingBox.width) boundingBox.width = bbox.width;
                    if (bbox.height > boundingBox.height) boundingBox.height = bbox.height;
                }
            }
        }
    }

    traverse(svg);

    return boundingBox;
}
```

This function takes an SVG element as input and returns an object with the coordinates of its bounding box. It uses a recursive function `traverse` to traverse the SVG tree and collect the coordinates of each element's bounding box.

To use this function, you can pass the root SVG element as an argument:

```javascript
const svgElement = document.querySelector('svg');
const boundingBox = getBoundingBox(svgElement);
console.log(boundingBox);
```

This will log the coordinates of the bounding box of the SVG element to the console.
